.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Vt BIO_ADDR
type is a wrapper around all types of socket addresses that OpenSSL
deals with, currently transparently supporting AF_INET, AF_INET6 and
AF_UNIX according to what's available on the platform at hand.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_new
creates a new unfilled
.Vt BIO_ADDR ,
to be used with routines that will fill it with information, such as
.Xr BIO_accept_ex 3 .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_copy
copies the contents of
.Fa src
into
.Fa dst .
Neither
.Fa src
or
.Fa dst
can be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_dup
creates a new
.Vt BIO_ADDR ,
with a copy of the address data in
.Fa ap .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_free
frees a
.Vt BIO_ADDR
created with
.Fn BIO_ADDR_new
or
.Fn BIO_ADDR_dup .
If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_clear
clears any data held within the provided
.Vt BIO_ADDR
and sets it back to an uninitialised state.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_rawmake
takes a protocol
.Fa family ,
a byte array of size
.Fa wherelen
with an address in network byte order pointed at by
.Fa where
and a port number in network byte order in
.Fa port
(except for the
.Sy AF_UNIX
protocol family, where
.Fa port
is meaningless and therefore ignored) and populates the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR
with them.
In case this creates a
.Sy AF_UNIX
.Vt BIO_ADDR ,
.Fa wherelen
is expected to be the length of the path string (not including the
terminating NUL, such as the result of a call to
.Xr strlen 3 ) .
Read on about the addresses in
.Sx RAW ADDRESSES
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_family
returns the protocol family of the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR .
The possible non-error results are one of the constants AF_INET,
AF_INET6 and AF_UNIX. It will also return AF_UNSPEC if the BIO_ADDR has
not been initialised.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_rawaddress
will write the raw address of the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR
in the area pointed at by
.Fa p
if
.Fa p
is non-NULL, and will set
.Sy *l
to be the amount of bytes the raw address takes up if
.Fa l
is non-NULL. A technique to only find out the size of the address is a
call with
.Fa p
set to
.Dv NULL .
The raw address will be in network byte order, most significant byte
first.
In case this is a
.Sy AF_UNIX
.Vt BIO_ADDR ,
.Fa l
gets the length of the path string (not including the terminating NUL,
such as the result of a call to
.Xr strlen 3 ) .
Read on about the addresses in
.Sx RAW ADDRESSES
below.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_rawport
returns the raw port of the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR .
The raw port will be in network byte order.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_hostname_string
returns a character string with the hostname of the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR .
If
.Fa numeric
is 1, the string will contain the numerical form of the address.
This only works for
.Vt BIO_ADDR
of the protocol families AF_INET and AF_INET6. The returned string has
been allocated on the heap and must be freed with
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_service_string
returns a character string with the service name of the port of the
given
.Vt BIO_ADDR .
If
.Fa numeric
is 1, the string will contain the port number.
This only works for
.Vt BIO_ADDR
of the protocol families AF_INET and AF_INET6. The returned string has
been allocated on the heap and must be freed with
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_path_string
returns a character string with the path of the given
.Vt BIO_ADDR .
This only works for
.Vt BIO_ADDR
of the protocol family AF_UNIX. The returned string has been allocated
on the heap and must be freed with
.Xr OPENSSL_free 3 .
.Sh RAW ADDRESSES
Both
.Fn BIO_ADDR_rawmake
and
.Fn BIO_ADDR_rawaddress
take a pointer to a network byte order address of a specific site.
Internally, those are treated as a pointer to
.Sy struct in_addr
(for
.Sy AF_INET Ns ),
.Sy struct in6_addr
(for
.Sy AF_INET6 )
or
.Sy char *
(for
.Sy AF_UNIX Ns ),
all depending on the protocol family the address is for.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The string producing functions
.Fn BIO_ADDR_hostname_string ,
.Fn BIO_ADDR_service_string
and
.Fn BIO_ADDR_path_string
will return
.Dv NULL
on error and leave an error indication on the OpenSSL error stack.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_ADDR_copy
returns 1 on success or 0 on error.
.Pp
All other functions described here return 0 or
.Dv NULL
when the information they should return isn't available.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr BIO_connect 3 ,
.Xr BIO_s_connect 3
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn BIO_ADDR_copy
and
.Fn BIO_ADDR_dup
were added in OpenSSL 3.2.
